    {"id": "patch_f418559e78da", "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata", "source_line": 160, "mechanism": "The read condition is incorrectly gated with write (line 160: replacement \"(read) && (write)\" would corrupt the read enable logic on the output clock domain, directly affecting output_axis_tdata at cycles 8-9 when reads should occur independently of writes. The original read condition at line 163 is simply 'read'; adding write creates a false dependency between clock domains that breaks FIFO throughput."},
    {"id": "patch_289187f12d1e", "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata", "source_line": 160, "mechanism": "Qualifying read with output_rst_sync3 at line 160 (replacement \"(read) && (output_rst_sync3)\") would block reads during the critical first cycles after reset deassertion. At cycle 4, output_rst_sync3 is still asserted (1), so read=0 prevents data_out_reg update, causing output_axis_tdata to remain 0 instead of becoming 1 at cycle 8."},
    {"id": "patch_ea9d6ca66e86", "failure_port": "input_axis_tready", "source_line": 105, "mechanism": "The patch adds '&& (input_rst_sync1)' to input_axis_tready at line 105, but input_rst_sync1 is 1 at cycle 4 (see trace: input_rst_sync1=1 at cycle 4). This forces input_axis_tready=0 at cycle 4, matching the actual=1 failure (expected 0). However it over-constrains: input_rst_sync1 stays 1 until cycle 5, so input_axis_tready would be 0 for cycles 4-5, breaking the handshake."},
    {"id": "patch_eb843a3e7514", "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata", "source_line": 160, "mechanism": "At line 160 (read assignment), qualifying read with '!read' is a tautology that makes read=0 always. This would freeze rd_ptr and data_out_reg at 0, causing output_axis_tdata=0 at cycles 8-9 (expected 1,2). The dependency shows first_line=159 (read definition) so this directly targets the read logic."},
    {"id": "patch_37040ee40903", "failure_port": "input_axis_tready", "source_line": 139, "mechanism": "At line 139 (write assignment), adding '&& (!input_rst_sync3)' would force write=0 while input_rst_sync3=1 (cycles 0-4). This prevents FIFO writes during early cycles, so no data enters the FIFO, causing output_axis_tdata to stay 0 at cycles 8-9. input_axis_tready=~full would remain 1 (since no writes occur), contradicting the actual=1 at cycle 4."},
    {"id": "patch_9d8c6fe52725", "failure_port": "input_axis_tready", "source_line": 105, "mechanism": "Adding '&& (output_rst_sync3)' to input_axis_tready at line 105: output_rst_sync3=1 at cycle 4, so input_axis_tready=0 at cycle 4 (matches actual=1 failure). But output_rst_sync3 clears at cycle 5, so input_axis_tready would pulse high then, whereas the trace shows input_axis_tready=1 at cycle 5 already. Over-constrains reset release timing."},
    {"id": "patch_74e85a269285", "failure_port": "input_axis_tready", "source_line": 110, "mechanism": "Changing async_rst to '(async_rst) && (input_rst_sync3)' at line 110 (input clock reset block) makes reset deassertion wait for input_rst_sync3=0. But input_rst_sync3 is driven by async_rst, creating a circular dependency: reset never deasserts, so wr_ptr/wr_ptr_gray stay 0, full=0, input_axis_tready=1 always — contradicting actual=1 at cycle 4."},
    {"id": "patch_c64900800719", "failure_port": "input_axis_tready", "source_line": 105, "mechanism": "Qualifying input_axis_tready with output_rst_sync2 at line 105: output_rst_sync2=1 at cycle 4 (one cycle after output_rst_sync1), so input_axis_tready=0 at cycle 4 (matches failure). But output_rst_sync2 clears at cycle 4, so input_axis_tready would rise at cycle 4 — conflicting with the need for input_axis_tready=0 at cycle 4 and 1 at cycle 5."}
  ],
  "explanation": "The top failures are output_axis_tdata mismatches at cycles 8-9 (expected 1,2; actual 0,0) and input_axis_tready at cycle 4 (expected 0; actual 1). The root cause is the reset synchronization between clock domains: input_rst_sync3 and output_rst_sync3 control when wr_ptr/rd_ptr exit reset. At cycle 4, input_rst_sync3=1 (still in reset) so write=0, but input_axis_tready=~full=1 (since wr_ptr=rd_ptr=0). The trace expects input_axis_tready=0 at cycle 4, implying full=1 — which requires wr_ptr!=rd_ptr. This suggests the reset release timing or pointer initialization is off by one cycle. Patches targeting read/write gating with reset sync signals (patch_f418559e78da, patch_289187f12d1e, patch_eb843a3e7514) directly corrupt the data flow to output_axis_tdata. Patches on input_axis_tready (patch_ea9d6ca66e86, patch_9d8c6fe52725, patch_c64900800719) match the cycle-4 symptom but over-constrain by using early reset sync stages. The most structurally relevant fixes would adjust when input_rst_sync3/output_rst_sync3 deassert, or the full/empty logic using synced pointers — but no candidate edits those exact lines. The ranked candidates are the least destructive to the observed failure ports."
}
